    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad Caliphate was established in 661 CE following the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> — the fourth and last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphs</a> and the cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> — when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muawiya-i">Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</a>, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and the most powerful military commander in the Islamic world, assumed the caliphate and transferred its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medina">Medina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a>. Muawiyah came from the Umayyad clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quraysh">Quraysh</a> tribe — the same clan that had been Muhammad's bitterest opponents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a> before the Prophet's victory in 630 CE — and his accession represented a fundamental transformation of the caliphal institution from an elected or consensus-based religious leadership to a hereditary Syro-Arab monarchy. The precedent Muawiyah established of appointing his son Yazid as successor was the immediate cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> (civil war, 680–692), in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> — Ali's son and Muhammad's grandson — was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> in 680 CE with his small party of followers by the forces of Yazid's governor, an event that became the defining martyrdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia Islam</a> and permanently divided the Muslim community into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia traditions. The Marwanid branch of the Umayyads, who recovered the caliphate from the competing Zubayrid claimants during the Second Fitna under Abd al-Malik (r. 685–705), proved more dynastically stable and administratively innovative than Muawiyah's direct descendants. Under Abd al-Malik and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> (r. 705–715), the caliphate reached its greatest territorial extent, with military campaigns simultaneously expanding into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> in what remains the most rapid territorial expansion in history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad economy was built on the agricultural surpluses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> — the most productive agricultural regions of the ancient world — supplemented by the revenues of a vast transcontinental trade network that connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> through Arab commercial intermediaries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a>'s fiscal system underwent a fundamental transformation under Abd al-Malik, who replaced the previous practice of maintaining separate Byzantine and Sasanian administrative and monetary systems in their respective former territories with a unified Islamic administrative and monetary system. The introduction of purely Arabic-language coinage (abandoning the Byzantine and Sasanian iconographic conventions and replacing them with Quranic inscriptions) standardised monetary transactions across the empire and asserted Islamic cultural authority over the commercial system. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jizya">jizya</a> tax on non-Muslim subjects (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dhimmi">dhimmis</a>) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kharaj">kharaj</a> land tax provided the primary fiscal revenues, creating a system in which conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> was partially disincentivised since it reduced the dhimmi population liable to jizya — a structural tension that would contribute to fiscal pressure as conversion rates increased under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Syrian Arab tribal elites constituted the primary beneficiaries of the Umayyad redistributive system: Arab warriors received stipends from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diwan">diwan</a> registers, while the agricultural and commercial productivity of conquered territories was taxed through existing administrative structures with minimal disruption to productive capacity. The Mediterranean trade — in grain, oil, textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papyrus">papyrus</a>, and luxury goods — continued to flow through the Umayyad period much as it had under Byzantine governance, with Arab merchants increasingly participating alongside the Greek, Coptic, and Syrian Christian commercial networks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad military was the most expansionist force of the seventh and early eighth centuries, achieving a pace of territorial acquisition that exceeded even the Macedonian conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in geographic scope if not in duration. The Arab armies that conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> were primarily light cavalry and infantry forces whose tactical flexibility, religious motivation, and the structural weaknesses of their opponents — the Byzantine and Sasanian empires had exhausted each other in decades of mutual warfare before the Arab expansion began — combined to produce a sequence of conquests that contemporaries found almost incomprehensible. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-yarmuk">Battle of the Yarmuk</a> (636 CE), in which Arab forces defeated the Byzantine army defending Syria, and the Battle of al-Qadisiyya (637 CE), in which Arab forces destroyed the Sasanian army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, were the defining engagements that opened the heartlands of two ancient empires. The subsequent Umayyad campaigns extended the conquests in both directions simultaneously: westward across North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a> to Spain (711 CE, the crossing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tariq-ibn-ziyad">Tariq ibn Ziyad</a>) and northward through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a>/Poitiers (732 CE) halted the expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a>; eastward through Khurasan into Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indus">Indus River</a> (711 CE, the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sind">Sind</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a>). The naval dimensions of Umayyad expansion included the first Arab naval force, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a> as governor of Syria, which conducted repeated raids on Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>. The failed sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> (674–678 and 717–718) demonstrated the limits of Arab naval and siege capability against the Byzantine capital's formidable defences and the devastating effectiveness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greek-fire">Greek fire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad governance system was characterised by an Arab tribal elite's attempt to administer an empire of extraordinary ethnic, religious, and linguistic diversity — a challenge it met with pragmatic institutional eclecticism rather than systematic administrative redesign. The existing Byzantine administrative framework in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#palestine">Palestine</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> was largely preserved, with Greek-speaking bureaucrats continuing to manage taxation, record-keeping, and local governance while Arabic gradually replaced Greek as the official language under Abd al-Malik's administrative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabisation">arabisation</a> reforms of the 690s. Similarly, the Sasanian administrative traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> were maintained through existing personnel, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dihqan">dihqan</a> (Sasanian rural aristocratic) class continuing to collect taxes from agricultural communities. This pragmatic approach — governing through existing institutions while asserting Islamic political supremacy — reduced administrative disruption but also created a governance structure in which the state's primary identification was with Arab Muslim identity rather than with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> as a universal community. The 'Arab supremacism' of Umayyad governance — the privileging of Arab tribal elites over non-Arab Muslims (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mawali">mawali</a>) in military stipends, administrative appointments, and social status — was the dynasty's most politically consequential structural choice, generating the deep resentment of Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a>, and other non-Arab converts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> would mobilise. The judiciary administered Islamic law through appointed judges (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qadi">qadis</a>), but customary and pre-Islamic legal traditions continued to govern much of daily life in non-Arab communities. The caliphal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a> adopted elements of Byzantine court ceremonial, creating an atmosphere of royal display that critics within the Muslim community condemned as incompatible with Islamic egalitarianism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad Caliphate's global historical impact was among the most consequential of any empire in history, permanently reshaping the religious, linguistic, and cultural geography of three continents within less than a century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> ended the Byzantine control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a>, transforming territories that had been Christian and Greek-speaking for centuries into predominantly Arabic-speaking Muslim societies — a linguistic and religious transformation that proved irreversible across nearly the entire conquered territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, and North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>, which had been centres of Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> for centuries, became predominantly Muslim within two to three generations of the conquest, though substantial Christian and Jewish minority communities survived in most regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamisation">Islamisation</a> of Persia — accomplished gradually across the Umayyad and early Abbasid periods — integrated Persian cultural and intellectual traditions into Islamic civilisation in a synthesis that would define Islamic theology, philosophy, and literature for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Arab conquest of Spain</a> (711–719 CE) established Islamic political authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> for nearly eight centuries, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> civilisation whose philosophical, scientific, and literary achievements would transmit Greek learning to medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> translations. The diffusion of Arabic as a language of governance, commerce, religion, and scholarship across a region stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> created a cultural and intellectual community of unprecedented geographic extent, making Arabic the most influential language in world history after Latin in the dissemination of pre-modern knowledge. The establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> as the majority religion across the Middle East and North Africa — a consequence primarily of the Umayyad conquests — shaped the political, cultural, and religious development of this vast region for the subsequent fourteen centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Umayyad Caliphate's fall in 750 CE was the product of structural contradictions embedded in its founding political choices that accumulated over a century until they produced a revolutionary coalition too broad to suppress. The central contradiction was the dynasty's identification with Arab tribal supremacy in an empire that was predominantly non-Arab: as the proportion of the Muslim community consisting of non-Arab converts (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mawali">mawali</a>) grew — particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> — the gap between the egalitarian theology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> and the hierarchical practice of Umayyad governance became increasingly intolerable. The mawali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khorasan">Khorasan</a>, the most densely converted and militarily significant non-Arab Muslim population, formed the social base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>. The religious dissatisfaction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia</a> Muslims, who had never accepted Umayyad legitimacy after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>, provided additional revolutionary motivation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berber-revolt">Berber revolts</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a> (739–743 CE), triggered by Arab officials' refusal to divide conquered land equally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> converts who had fought alongside them, demonstrated that the empire's peripheral populations were prepared to contest Umayyad authority militarily. The internal crisis deepened when fierce tribal rivalries — between the Yemeni (Qahtan) and northern Arab (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qays">Qays</a>) tribal factions — paralysed the Umayyad military at precisely the moment when external and internal threats required unified response. The Abbasid Revolution, launched from Khorasan in 747 CE under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, swept westward with extraordinary speed: by 750 CE, the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-great-zab">Battle of the Great Zab</a>, pursued into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, and killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> executed virtually the entire Umayyad family — a single survivor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> — and transferred the caliphate to a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>, marking the end of Umayyad rule and the beginning of the Abbasid era.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Umayyad Caliphate's supreme historical achievement was the creation of the largest empire yet assembled in the space of a single century — an expansion so rapid and so permanent in its religious and linguistic consequences that it remains the foundational political event of the Islamic world.</em></p>

